  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Find the global minimum of the function $f(x,y)=xy-x-y$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,1)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices.",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212772/assets/7fe1aa21de9b6354e561eb1e82f6c83b25c71770470dc8501e6b491e35e2c391.png)\n\n \n\nSince the point $(1,1)$ is in the interior of $D$, we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(2,2),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =(1)(1)-1-1 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ f(0,0) & =(0)(0)-0-0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)(0)-2-0 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ f(2,2) & =(2)(2)-2-2 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(0,2) & =(0)(2)-0-2 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-2$ at the points $(2,0)$and $(0,2),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $0$ at the points $(0,0)$ and $(2,2).$",
